From 990cfe99c987bb8f4732deda5249599ddec43604 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 22:51:45 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] tools/dvbridge: Dolby Vision Low-Latency: the picture mapped
 to the display, packed without metadata

A display that takes Dolby Vision only as Low-Latency ("player-led": the LG C5 in its game mode, a Sony
of 2020) expects the player to map the picture to it and to send it as YCbCr 4:2:2 at 12 bits, PQ in
BT.2020, with no display management metadata in it. The native renderer can now do that
(dvbridge_render_set_low_latency()):

- the picture is drawn mapped as dvbridge_render_hdr10() draws it with the same mapping (that of Dolby
  Vision as HDR10 to a display: the BT.2390 curve guided by each scene's level 1 metadata, libplacebo's
  perceptual gamut mapping, the black of the source kept, nothing above the peak), to a gamut of the
  display's own (DVBRIDGE_HDR10_GAMUT_CUSTOM: the primaries of its Dolby VSVDB). The VSVDB gives them
  in steps of 1/256, which can put one a little past BT.2020 (the Sony's red, by 0.0013 in xy): such a
  primary is taken onto BT.2020's edge, up to 0.006 (dvbridge_hdr10_custom_xy()); one farther out is
  no gamut of a BT.2020 signal and the mapping is refused;
- the composed way only: the direct way of the converted transport writes the metadata bits, and the
  source transport sends the composer's picture;
- packed by the converted transport's packer without a packet (dvbridge_gl_pack_no_metadata()): the
  layout of the tunnel, which the kernel then signals as YCbCr 4:2:2;
- an output hook of its own, with its own signatures: the bars black, the clamp to the display's peak
  and, for a first look at a display, a test pattern in place of the picture
  (dvbridge_render_set_ll_pattern()). The shader of the other renders does not change;
- the check of the bytes (dvbridge_render_reference()) and the warm-up follow it.

Measured with the software renderer (Mesa's zink on lavapipe), on three pictures of a profile 5
stream, for the Low-Latency targets of the user's LG C5 and of that Sony:
- the picture is that of Dolby Vision as HDR10 mapped to the same display, to the bit, in the active
  area; nothing above the display's peak, also with a level 1 maximum lowered to 300 cd/m2 in a copy
  of the metadata (the clamp is what holds it then); the bars black, also over grey ones;
- every luma and chroma code of the transport is that of the picture in double precision, but for
  codes one off at a rounding boundary (the GPU's single precision); back to R'G'B' it is 0.5 codes of
  4095 off on average (the same picture as RGB at 10 bits: 0.9), more where the two pixels of a pair
  differ in colour (4:2:2: up to 685 codes on the edges of a chart);
- the test pattern's codes are exact;
- with Low-Latency off, the transport and its picture are those of the renderer before, byte for byte.
libplacebo's perceptual gamut mapping takes the colours toward the display's gamut, not all of them
inside it: on these wide-gamut pictures the farthest goes from 0.11 to 0.012 to 0.021 in xy (on the
chart, to 0.002). What is left is clipped by the display, as for Dolby Vision as HDR10 mapped to it.
